      "id": 1,
      "text": "Myrtlewood takes its name from a grove of crepe myrtle trees that grew near the original town site during its 1957 incorporation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Myrtlewood%2C_Alabama",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The 1902 completion of the Meridian and Bigbee Railroad line through Myrtlewood sparked a boom, bringing rail service to the town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/myrtlewood/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Myrtlewood’s post office has been operating since 1886, long before the town’s official incorporation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Myrtlewood%2C_Alabama",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Perched 100 feet above the Tombigbee River valley, Myrtlewood enjoys a modest elevation that protects it from flooding.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Myrtlewood%2C_Alabama",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
